before:
Object.create(parent) makes an object with parent as its prototype.
```javascript
const defaults = { color: "teal" };
const badge = Object.create(defaults);
console.log(badge.color);
```
The lookup finds color on defaults.

Writing a normal own data property can shadow an inherited one.
```javascript
badge.color = "gold";
console.log(badge.color, defaults.color);
```
These read gold and teal.

Object.hasOwn(object, key) tests for an own property. The in operator also finds inherited properties.

```javascript
const base = {badge:"Explorer"};
const item = Object.create(base);
console.log(Object.hasOwn(item, "badge"));
console.log("badge" in item);
item.badge = "Researcher";
console.log(Object.hasOwn(item, "badge"));
```
The outputs are false, true, true. Object.hasOwn receives the object and property key and returns a Boolean without changing it. Assigning item.badge creates the own property in this example.

after:
An own property can hide an inherited property with the same name. This is called shadowing.
```javascript
const defaults = { color: "teal" };
const badge = Object.create(defaults);
badge.color = "gold";
console.log(badge.color, defaults.color);
```
Output:
```text
gold teal
```
The badge gets its own color. The default color stays unchanged.

`Object.hasOwn(object, key)` checks only the object itself. The `in` operator checks both own and inherited properties.
```javascript
const base = {badge:"Explorer"};
const item = Object.create(base);
console.log(Object.hasOwn(item, "badge"));
console.log("badge" in item);
item.badge = "Researcher";
console.log(Object.hasOwn(item, "badge"));
```
Output:
```text
false
true
true
```
At first, `badge` is inherited. The assignment then creates an own property on `item`.
